I agree with your subject line.  I've appended my letter to the editor...
>TECHNOLOGICAL INHERITANCE BELONGS TO ALL OF US
>In October's "Technology Review," Gar Alperovitz argues that the $8 billion
>in Bill Gates' bank account was derived through thousands of links in a
>chain of technological development, largely funded by government research.
>"Plainly put, the way we allocate the benefits of present and past economic
>activity that stem from the technological inheritance is irrational and
>unjust." Alperovitz calls for a new economic system based on the notion of
>common inheritance of wealth generated through government-funded R&D.
>(Technology Review 10/94 p.31)

To: technology-review-letters@mit.edu
From: vikbob@halcyon.com (Viktor Lawryniuk)
Subject: Not Such a Big Secret, Actually

September 19, 1994

To:  Steven J. Marcus

Re:  "First Line" in October 1994 issue of TR

Dear Mr. Marcus:

I believe the answer to your advertising dilemma can be found inside the
same issue in which you write.  First review your feature *Distributing Our
Technological Inheritance*.  With this scenario in mind, begin a
promotional campaign aimed at the large magazine-publishing firms to
convince them to distribute their earnings more equitably with
"second-tier" publishers such as yourselves.  After all, their publications
are merely variations on the same concept "pioneered over generations and
contributed to by numerous people" as is yours.  Perhaps in a show of good
faith, MIT could similarly redistribute its endowment...I know of many
colleges whose intellectual "pebble resting on a mountain of previous
achievements" is, sadly, not the recipient of alumni contributions to the
same degree as MIT.

If this appeal to Cambridge common sense fails, please turn to page 72 of
the same issue and reacquaint yourself with the "Phenomena" column, *My
Mind Explores the Universe*.  (Please insist Gar Alperovitz read this
column as well.)  Using your editorial intuition, I suspect you may begin
to realize that "Gar Alperovitz" is actually a pseudonym for one of the
letter writers mentioned by David Brittan.  Unfortunately, though attuned
you are to the cranky in science and technology, you seem totally
unprepared to recognize truly idiotic statist babble when it arrives from
outside of that realm.

I believe far too many economic cranks have been sneaking into your
publication during my short tenure as a reader.  (This is a gift
subscription.)  Sadly, though this doesn't spoil the entire magazine for
me, it does leave me with a crisis of conscience:  I cannot voluntarily
support a publication that works to limit my individual freedom.  I suspect
many of your "second-tier" potential advertisers may also feel that they
don't need to support a publication that promotes a dubious future for
them.

Perhaps you could make an effort to balance the "government is our god"
approach with something more friendly to the free market and individual
rights.  Karl Popper just died, perhaps you could run something about him.
It would be a start.

Sincerely,
