Extropes:

Has anyone else here on the list been following the Baltic Sea Ferry
tragedy? For those who have, the latest news is that within the
last few hours, the wreck of the M/S Estonia has been found. (the Finnish 
survey vessel "Suunta" localized the wreck of the "Estonia" at 1530 GMT).
Now maybe they can find out what caused the accident (only one engineer 
in the hold saw the sequence of events that may have caused it).

>From the balt-l list:

"For the many countries that share the Baltic Sea it is indeed a
sea of co-operation. The Baltic Sea is one of the busiest shipping
routes in the world, and the tragedy will be deeply felt by the
millions of people in the region who each year use ferry travel in
their everyday lives, for work and pleasure, and can recount their
experience of safe travel. A clear example of the ferry traffic in
the region is highlighted by the approximate 350,000 people who,
in 1993 *alone*, arrived or departed through Estonia's ports
without incident. When this number is added with those people
travelling from other coastal capitals and cities, from Copenhagen
to St Petersburg, the number of passengers safely crossing the sea
will equal millions every year."


Close to 1000 are presumed dead. This is the biggest peace-time sea disaster 
in history. Only the wartime sinkings in the Baltic Sea  by torpedo (by 
Soviet submarines) of the refugee ships "Wilhelm Gusaff" and "Goya" top 
this figure - over seven thousand drowned on each of those sinkings.

I'm thankful that my relatives or I weren't on that ship. I've
crossed the Baltic many times on various ferries, and my cousin was
a DJ on the M/S Estonia just last year. For those of you who lost someone
you know, my deepest regrets.


Amara


