Well, I stood on the deck of the Estonia 50 days ago, on the same Tallin 
- Stockholm leg of it's trip.
  
We were playing with the lifeboats before departure, 
trying to figure out how the pullies worked to lower the boats into the 
water.

If you've ever been on one of these ferries, you know they are just 
huge floating bars.  Tourists pour on board, and pour the cheap booze 
(the taxes in Sweden are some huge percent on booze, so this is more of a 
problem on the Estonia-Sweden leg thanks to the duty free shop, which is
really a duty free package store) for the entire night.  The Swedes 
knew what the ferry Estonia was all about, they breathalized me, along with
all the passengers who drove off the ferry, as soon as we drove off the boat.
In my dreams I see hundreds of drunken tourists trampling each other for 
the life boats we examined so casually only 7 weeks ago.  I picture the 
rich types, who had the cabins in the middle decks, trapped by a boat 
that sunk in 15 minutes, stuck in a slow death in some air pocket that 
such sinkings create.

I have this picture of us on the ferry, the ferry which now lies at the 
bottom of the Baltic.  A bunch of crew members are in the background.  Ghosts.

My regrets to all the families of loved ones.

