>> From: Bryan Leithiser <BLEITHISER@DELPHI.COM>
>> Subject:      Recursion, science and God
>> X-To:         ssrel-l@utkvm1.utk.edu
>> To: Multiple recipients of list SSREL-L <SSREL-L%UTKVM1.BITNET@uga.cc.uga.edu>
>> 
>>      As this is a list on the scientific study of religon I
>> offer the following for your comments.
>>      Recursion is a theory in mathematics. It is particularly
>> associated with information theory. Recursion refers to the
>> "repeated" application of a mathematical algorithm to produce new
>> information. The best illustration of this is its use in a computer
>> game called the Game of Life. A computer is programmed to
>> "repeatedly" apply a mathematical formula to a given set of values.
>> For instance, using the monitor screen as a field of dots, 80 dots
>> wide and 24 dots long. Placing dots at certain locations on the
>> screen, the algorithm uses the information on these dots to create
>> a new pattern. As an example, if a dot has an other dot to its left
>> then the new configuration of these two dots will be to moved to
>> the next line and will add three new dots on the lines below. The
>> computer takes algorithms like this and produces a new pattern on
>> the screen. Then takes this new pattern, and  applies the same
>> algorithms again and again. The patterns that appear on the screen
>> become increasingly complex and varied. Indeed the patterns almost
>> take on a "life" of their own with images growing, flourishing and
>> dying and new patterns emerging.
>>      This idea of increasing complexity can be seen in nature,
>> indeed it can be seen in the universe. For this is how the complex
>> structure of universe evolved. If the universe began as a single
>> ball of quarks that exploded into the Big Bang and the only forces
>> at work in the first moments or even the first few billion years,
>> were the forces of gravity, the nuclear weak force, the nuclear
>> strong force and electromagnetism, then perhaps it was the
>> recursive application of these four forces on the matter of the
>> universe which created the enormous complexity of the our world and
>> indeed ourselves, conscious, rational beings.
>>      Science as discovered the algorithms (physical laws) but has
>> ignored the recursive application of these algorithms. Albert
>> Einstein once mused "...if God had any choice in creating the
>> universe? Whether the laws of physics could have been any
>> different?" One must admit that if the nuclear weak force were any
>> weaker or stronger the universe could not exist in its present
>> state and we would not exist. The same could be said of any of the
>> other three basic laws of the physical universe. But the recursive
>> application of these laws could have produced any number of
>> different patterns of this same universe. The universe could have
>> formed into any number of infinitely different patterns.
>>      We must now contemplate the fact that science has done a
>> marvelous job. It has probed the very heart of the universe and
>> discovered its secrets. But science as ignored the recursive
>> patterns that evolved into this complex universe. These patterns
>> may indeed be too complex for science to determine.
>>      Science uses the rules of logic to conduct is search for the
>> laws of the universe. But logic contains within itself paradoxical
>> limitations that seem to ensure that we can never comprehend the
>> totality of universe. Werner Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principal has
>> shown us that all measurable quantities are subject to
>> unpredictable flucuations making it impossible to determine their
>> exact values. The recursive patterns are too complex at the
>> subatomic level for science to comprehend. Science does have its
>> limitations, and the study of these recursive applications of the
>> physical laws may indeed be beyond the scope of science.
>>      The great discovery yet to made is the Grand Unified Field
>> Theory. This it has been claimed would be the crowning achievement
>> of science. But even if it were to be found, it would only increase
>> the mystery of how a single force applied at the time of the Big
>> bang created the marvelous complexity of this universe. How this
>> single force recursively applied gave birth to the four physical
>> laws and how these in turn produced other more complex laws and
>> patterns to evenually produce the earth and humankind. We who now
>> contemplate the universe itself.
>>      Perhaps these recursive movements are are too complex for
>> science, but we can ask, are these recursive patterns which have
>> created the world and us, the hand of God?  Shall we endow the cold
>> dead matter of universe with the power to arrange itself into
>> concsious rational beings and create an environment that just
>> happens to be suitable for us?  Or might we say that the
>> recursively created patterns just may have been consciously
>> designed.
>> 
